S1. SURFACE (a) — STRICT-ASSEMBLY COHERENCE.  6 defects (3 GAP, 3 minor)
================================================================================

Method.  For every "replace"/"insert" directive in r1-r12 I located the quoted
FROM-text in the round it claims to modify, fixed the span end, assembled, and
applied two tests: (i) DUPLICATION — does the replacement text re-supply
material that lies OUTSIDE the declared span? (ii) ORPHAN — does the assembly
leave a sentence, display, or table row without its governing context, or a
stale assertion that the replacement was written to retire?

The note's own span conventions, established by its own directives, fix the
narrow reading and are used throughout below:
  * A tag name denotes the BOXED DISPLAY ONLY.  Precedent: R2-3, "Replace
    (C6-residue) by: \[boxed display\]" — (C6-residue) there is exactly the box,
    not its lead-in sentence.
  * When a directive means to take a display with a sentence, it QUOTES the
    display.  Precedent: R7-1, R5-1b, R1-2b all quote the display inside the
    from-text.
  * When a directive means to take an adjacent non-quoted item it NAMES it.
    Precedent: R2-4, "(C6-clearance) AND ITS FOLLOWING COLLECTION SENTENCE".
  * r12 itself (R12-4) ruled that a span must be declared through its actual
    end, and that a replacement re-supplying text past its declared end is a
    defect to be repaired by re-declaring the span.

